720 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (All sorts of emotions flooded me, from anger to sadness to self-pity. Watching my mother stoically and single-handedly organize the final rites of her one living parent within the shackles of the strict lockdown rules, it suddenly hit me - what if I had lost my mum? I grew up in that moment as I tried to get out of my wallowing to ease whatever little I could for my mother - even if it was just warding off the constant phone calls or fetching her a glass of water. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
721 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
722 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The pandemic, a cruel puppeteer, had yanked on the strings of lockdown, severing the connection with friends who could have offered a comforting embrace. The digital world was a flimsy medium failing to transmit the warmth of a hug or the solace of a shared tear. Realising how the virtual world and remote learning had isolated us, I now badly wanted to be around my friends and family- People! Anyone who gets me feels my pain and can just be with me. I was only twelve then, but I learned the immense value of human interaction. It's not the happy occasions that define us; I think the lows bring out our better sides. This gift of understanding the other person is a blessing that is irreplaceable by technology. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
723 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (It became more apparent to me as people risked themselves to come over to pay their condolences. A single act of caring, a simple question of "Are you okay?" can power a cascade of support, a testament to the interconnectedness of human experience. I learned to care less for my gadgets and more for my people. My Nani, in her absence, had, as always, left behind a gift, introducing me to my empathic side. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
