758 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Empathy, the ability to comprehend and share another's feelings, transcends fleeting trends. Despite its contemporary perception, there are countless stories in ancient literature; a testament to its enduring importance. This ability to connect with experiences beyond our own isn't confined to a single culture or historical period. In a world often marked by differences, empathy offers people the power to build connections across borders and backgrounds. By embracing this quality, we can build bridges of compassion, not just within our own communities, but across the vast cultural and geographical landscapes that define humanity. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
759 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Leaders throughout history have demonstrated empathy as a potent force for good. Look no further than Mahatma Gandhi, the champion of India's independence movement. Guided by the philosophy of Ahimsa \(non-violence and oneness\), Gandhi advocated for the rights of his people and independence \(1947\). He preached compassion towards all beings, striving to mend divides between religious groups. His iconic quote, "You must be the change you want to see in the world," embodies his belief in empathy as a catalyst for social transformation and peaceful coexistence. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
