1125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The first \(popular\) definition makes us think about a similar emotion: pity. At first, it seems similar to empathy, for it shares in common the feature of feeling \(yourself\) the feelings of another. However, pity only involves doing this when the other person is in an unfortunate state of mind. Our happiness is reduced slightly by their situation, but notably, we do not do anything to redeem the situation. On the surface, this seems similar to empathy but has the wrong sentiment, and so is not similar. Empathy involves feeling the feelings of the other, regardless of their mental state. If they are feeling unhappy, we feel bad with them and not for them. The other side of empathy is feeling happy for the other person when they are in a good situation, even if they are not. This characteristic is what makes empathy so powerful, more than pity can ever be. While pity reduces the total happiness among people, empathy increases it; empathy makes the other person feel better \(which in turn makes our conscience feel better\) when they are unhappy and makes us feel better when the other party is happy! But does it always do so? If we are empathetic from a sense of duty, eventually we may tire it could even lead to us becoming annoyed when someone is unhappy \(which is, of course, not empathetic!\). On the other hand, if we are empathetic for the express purpose of making others happy, it would naturally make us happier too. )
/E (The first \(popular\) definition makes us think about a similar emotion: pity. At first, it seems similar to empathy, for it shares in common the feature of feeling \(yourself\) the feelings of another. However, pity only involves doing this when the other person is in an unfortunate state of mind. Our happiness is reduced slightly by their situation, but notably, we do not do anything to redeem the situation. On the surface, this seems similar to empathy but has the wrong sentiment, and so is not similar. Empathy involves feeling the feelings of the other, regardless of their mental state. If they are feeling unhappy, we feel bad with them and not for them. The other side of empathy is feeling happy for the other person when they are in a good situation, even if they are not. This characteristic is what makes empathy so powerful, more than pity can ever be. While pity reduces the total happiness among people, empathy increases it; empathy makes the other person feel better \(which in turn makes our conscience feel better\) when they are unhappy and makes us feel better when the other party is happy! But does it always do so? If we are empathetic from a sense of duty, eventually we may tire it could even lead to us becoming annoyed when someone is unhappy \(which is, of course, not empathetic!\). On the other hand, if we are empathetic for the express purpose of making others happy, it would naturally make us happier too. )
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (True friendships need empathy because it puts both parties on a level playing field, revealing the innermost emotional qualities of each. Even if they have something in common and get along well, neither of them can know how the other thinks without empathy. It ties people together without a specific reason, transcending rationality. Empathy is giving something unconditionally. )
/E (True friendships need empathy because it puts both parties on a level playing field, revealing the innermost emotional qualities of each. Even if they have something in common and get along well, neither of them can know how the other thinks without empathy. It ties people together without a specific reason, transcending rationality. Empathy is giving something unconditionally. )
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Families lead to relationships in a similar vein, if stronger. For those of the same generation, perhaps empathy is easiest because they go through the same ordeals. For example, yesterday when I saw my younger brother being treated unfairly, I remembered going through the same situation when I was his age, without anyone to support me, and so I silently supported him. It rested my conscience, and I can only assume that my brother was slightly comforted. )
/E (Families lead to relationships in a similar vein, if stronger. For those of the same generation, perhaps empathy is easiest because they go through the same ordeals. For example, yesterday when I saw my younger brother being treated unfairly, I remembered going through the same situation when I was his age, without anyone to support me, and so I silently supported him. It rested my conscience, and I can only assume that my brother was slightly comforted. )
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [6]
>>
